WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.879
Slip on those Gauntlets of Submission and
get ready to tussle the Wonder Woman 1984

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:10.640
trailer is here, and boy, oh boy, it's bursting
at the seams with action and a whole lot of

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.640
heart.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.560
Let's take a look at what this feast of a
trailer gave us after so many months of build-up.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.240
Wonder Woman 1984 is set at an interesting
time in Diana Prince's on-screen history:

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:27.119
33 years before Justice League, which was
set in the then-present day of 2017, and 66

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:31.050
years after the original Wonder Woman, which
took place across various European countries

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.820
in the year 1918.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.350
As the title tells us, the sequel takes Diana's
story to 1984, when she's living in America.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:44.109
In the Wonder Woman 1984 trailer, framed pictures
on Diana's mantle imply that she's moved overseas

00:00:44.109 --> 00:00:49.219
to connect with members of Steve Trevor's
family hoping to, in what small ways she can,

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:53.739
remember the man she loved who seemingly died
at the end of the first Wonder Woman film.

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:58.400
But was Diana's presumed contact with Steve's
family overt or covert?

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.860
Did she keep in touch with her other friends
like Etta, or Charlie, or Chief Napi?

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.130
By the '80s, all these people would likely
be long dead.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.590
But Steve Trevor well, he's a different story.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:13.659
Yes, Chris Pine really is back as Steve Trevor.

00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:18.520
He's not dead, and, to quote director Patty
Jenkins, he's "not a gimmick" he's here to

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.380
really hang out in a future he couldn't possibly
imagine, decked out in windbreakers and fanny

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.380
packs.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.540
"It's all art."

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.320
Steve stops in front of trash can.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.439
"That's just a trash can."

00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:32.590
"This is just a trash can — yeah, yeah,
yeah."

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:37.130
The trailer dances around exactly how Steve
comes back from the presumed dead, but the

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:41.759
footage does give a lot of credence to the
Wonder Woman 1984 rumors reported over the

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:46.640
last few months that there exists a supernaturally
powered artifact that brings Steve to the

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.640
current timeline.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.010
Though Pine has previously admitted he doesn't
get a whole lot of action sequences in Wonder

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:56.760
Woman 1984, he seems to be a huge part of
the narrative overall considering he spends

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.049
a ton of time with Diana in the trailer.

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:03.539
It's sweet and fulfilling to see Diana and
Steve realize the romance they didn't quite

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:08.789
get to in Wonder Woman, but all of it is inherently
tinged with a sense of doom because we know

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:13.370
Steve didn't appear in Justice League and
Diana was mocked by that film's antagonist

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:14.480
over his death.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.250
Whatever happens, let's hope Diana gets the
longer goodbye she deserves if Steve must

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.060
disappear into the ether of time once more.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.730
As the Wonder Woman 1984 trailer shows, Diana
seems to still be working within the world

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:33.540
of art and antiquities, and she becomes acquainted
with an archaeologist named Barbara Ann Minerva.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.170
Kristen Wiig portrays Barbara, who, over the
course of the film, will take on the superpowered

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:39.739
mantle of Cheetah.

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:45.129
The Wonder Woman 1984 trailer didn't provide
any shots of Barbara post-Cheetah transformation,

00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:49.430
and instead focused on presenting her as a
new friend to Diana.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.909
At the start of the footage, the two women
are seen chatting at a table, talking about

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:55.319
whether they've been in love.

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:57.489
"Have you ever been in love?"

00:02:57.489 --> 00:02:58.850
"A long, long time ago."

00:02:58.850 --> 00:02:59.850
"You?"

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:00.850
"So many times.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:01.850
Yeah.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:02.850
All the time."

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:03.850
"You?"

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:08.019
Barbara has a magnificent, punkish '80s energy
to her — she wears studded animal print

00:03:08.019 --> 00:03:12.440
leather jackets, has big frizzy hair, and
rocks round nerdy glasses.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:17.670
As an archeologist, she and Diana have a very
natural path to meeting through a shared industry,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.970
and are seen attending what appears to be
the same slick parties with dignitaries in

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:26.629
what we assume to be Washington D.C., where
most major antiquities for the United States

00:03:26.629 --> 00:03:28.180
are kept in the Smithsonian.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:34.220
In the DC comics, Cheetah is a major antagonist
against Diana, so the Wonder Woman 1984 trailer

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:38.000
presenting them as new friends sets the stage
for a tragedy.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.689
Gal Gadot [guh-DUT] also might've let something
slip at CCXP when she mentioned in passing

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:47.269
that Diana wants to save some of the antagonists
she confronts in the film.

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:51.170
Rumors have been floating around for a while
that Barbara will become Cheetah as a result

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:55.970
of slick businessman Maxwell Lord more on
him in a bit granting her wish to be like

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.220
Wonder Woman, whom she admires more than anyone
else.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.500
According to the rumors, Barbara's new powers
will frighten Maxwell, who reaches out to

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.660
Diana for protection from Cheetah.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:10.769
Speculation also has it that Maxwell's bargain
for these services is using a mystical artifact

00:04:10.769 --> 00:04:16.150
in his possession to bring Steve Trevor back
to Diana the greatest wish to grant of all.

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:20.330
If this is true, Cheetah and Wonder Woman
are headed for a collision of circumstance,

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:26.440
and it would indeed be in Diana's nature to
pity the afflicted and want to help her.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:32.160
Circling back to Maxwell Lord, he's the central
villain of Wonder Woman 1984, and The Mandalorian

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.100
actor Pedro Pascal portrays him.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:40.270
Maxwell Lord in the DC Comics canon is a CEO
of some scientific mega-corporation a lá

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:45.790
Lex Luthor, but Maxwell Lord in Wonder Woman
1984 is "the king of infomercials," presented

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.850
as a continued nod to the particular gods
we served during that decade.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:54.840
His ultimate desire and the plan to achieve
it isn't explicitly stated in the trailer.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.510
Still, one of his many ominous lines in the
Wonder Woman 1984 trailer is:

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:04.280
"Think about finally having everything you
always wanted."

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:08.160
… which is no doubt a reflection of his
own ulterior motive.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.500
Like every evil salesman, Maxwell is a master
manipulator, and will utilize this skill to

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.380
get exactly what he wants at any cost.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:23.190
One of the best treats in the Wonder Woman
1984 trailer was saved for last: a brand-new,

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.000
flashy suit of golden armor with wings.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.680
After watching Diana go toe-to-toe with an
actual, capital-g God in 2017's Wonder Woman

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.240
without needing heavy armor, we can only wonder
just what she's getting up to in Wonder Woman

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.350
1984 to need something so resplendent.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:43.630
Comics refer to the shiny get-up as her Golden
Eagle armor, but they don't provide a whole

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:46.610
lot of clues or a special enemy it was used
for.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:50.880
Historically speaking, Diana simply needs
it for conflicts in which she needs to be

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.870
a soldier, as opposed to the champion of love
she prefers to be.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:59.340
That considered, there's a possibility that
the armor could be used as a remix of a comic

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.130
plot from 2016.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:05.440
In it, Wonder Woman has a final confrontation
with Cheetah, but rather than showing up to

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:09.770
kill her, Wonder Woman takes a brutal onslaught
from her former friend.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:14.560
Ultimately, she embraces Cheetah with love
and peace for two reasons: Cheetah is a villain

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.720
created by the abuses of others and isn't
inherently evil; and Diana always wants to

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.710
rehabilitate rather than kill.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:26.080
A suit of armor would fit the bill for that
excellently, and make for a nice reversal

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.220
of the trope that heroes only need armor for
ruthless fights to the death.

